// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 11): technology.
ADDART({id:"malware",t:"Computer Viruses and Malware",c:"Technology",i:"🦠",w:"Malware",r:["cybersecurity","hacking","passwords","operatingsystems","email","ransomware","firewalls"],
p:[["Harmful software","Malware is software designed to damage computers, steal data or demand money."],["Many kinds","Viruses, worms, trojans, spyware and ransomware are types of malware."],["Protection","Updates, backups and caution with links and attachments help protect computers."]],
l:"Malware, short for malicious software, is any program designed to harm computers, steal information, spy on users or take control of devices. Computer viruses attach themselves to other files and spread when those files are shared; worms spread on their own across networks; trojans disguise themselves as useful programs; spyware secretly collects information; and ransomware locks or encrypts files and demands payment to restore them. The idea of self-copying programs was explored in the 1940s by mathematician John von Neumann. Early examples include the experimental Creeper program in 1971 and the Elk Cloner virus, written by a 15-year-old for Apple II computers in 1982. The Morris worm of 1988 infected about a tenth of computers on the early internet. Email worms such as ILOVEYOU in 2000 spread to millions of computers in days. Ransomware attacks, such as WannaCry in 2017, have disrupted hospitals, businesses and governments, and the 2010 Stuxnet worm sabotaged Iranian nuclear equipment. Security experts recommend keeping software updated, using antivirus protection, backing up files and being careful with email attachments and links.",
s:[["Types of malware","Besides viruses and worms, malware includes keyloggers that record keystrokes, botnets that turn infected computers into networks controlled by criminals, adware that floods users with advertisements and cryptojackers that secretly use computers to mine cryptocurrency."],["Ransomware","Ransomware gangs encrypt victims’ data and demand payment, usually in cryptocurrency, and often threaten to publish stolen data. The 2021 attack on the Colonial Pipeline caused fuel shortages along the US East Coast. Governments discourage paying ransoms, which can fund further crime."],["ILOVEYOU","The ILOVEYOU worm, released from the Philippines in May 2000, arrived as an email with the subject ‘ILOVEYOU’ and an attachment that appeared to be a love letter. When opened, it overwrote files and emailed itself to everyone in the victim’s address book, causing billions of dollars in damage worldwide."],["Defenses","Antivirus programs detect malware by recognizing known code and suspicious behavior. Operating system updates fix security holes. Organizations train staff to spot phishing, use multi-factor authentication and keep offline backups so they can recover without paying ransoms."]],
k:[["Main types","Viruses, worms, trojans, spyware, ransomware"],["Early virus","Elk Cloner, 1982"],["First major internet worm","Morris worm, 1988"],["Famous email worm","ILOVEYOU, 2000"],["Major ransomware attack","WannaCry, 2017"]],
d:[["1971","Creeper, an experimental self-copying program"],["1982","Elk Cloner virus for the Apple II"],["1988","Morris worm infects the early internet"],["2000","ILOVEYOU worm spreads worldwide"],["2010","Stuxnet worm discovered"],["2017","WannaCry ransomware attack"],["2021","Colonial Pipeline ransomware attack"]],
s2:[["Stuxnet","Stuxnet, discovered in 2010, was a highly sophisticated worm that damaged centrifuges used to enrich uranium in Iran by making them spin out of control while showing normal readings to operators. It is widely believed to have been created by the United States and Israel."],["The Morris worm","Robert Tappan Morris, a graduate student, released his worm in 1988 to measure the size of the internet, but a programming error made it copy itself repeatedly, slowing thousands of computers. He became the first person convicted under the US Computer Fraud and Abuse Act and later became a professor at MIT."]],
sp:["Why ‘virus’?","Computer scientist Fred Cohen popularized the term ‘computer virus’ in 1983, comparing self-copying programs to biological viruses that infect cells and use them to make copies of themselves."],
g:[["Malware","Software designed to cause harm"],["Ransomware","Malware that locks data and demands payment"],["Worm","Malware that spreads by itself across networks"],["Trojan","Malware disguised as a useful program"]],
f:["Elk Cloner was written by a 15-year-old in 1982.","The ILOVEYOU worm spread to millions of computers.","Backups help victims recover from ransomware."],
fun:"Elk Cloner, one of the first computer viruses, displayed a short poem on infected Apple II computers every 50th time they started up."});

ADDART({id:"captcha",t:"CAPTCHAs",c:"Technology",i:"🤖",w:"CAPTCHA",r:["ai","cybersecurity","compvision","worldwideweb","passwords"],
p:[["Are you human?","CAPTCHAs are tests that websites use to tell people from bots."],["Wavy letters and traffic lights","They ask users to type distorted text or pick images."],["An AI arms race","As AI improves, CAPTCHAs must become harder or invisible."]],
l:"A CAPTCHA, which stands for ‘Completely Automated Public Turing test to tell Computers and Humans Apart’, is a challenge that websites use to check whether a user is a human rather than an automated program, or bot. CAPTCHAs help stop bots from creating fake accounts, posting spam, buying up concert tickets or guessing passwords. The term was coined in 2003 by Luis von Ahn, Manuel Blum and colleagues at Carnegie Mellon University. Early CAPTCHAs showed distorted letters and numbers that were easy for people to read but hard for computers. In reCAPTCHA, launched by von Ahn in 2007 and bought by Google in 2009, users typed words from scanned old books and newspapers that computers could not read, helping to digitize millions of texts. Later versions asked users to click images containing traffic lights, buses or crosswalks, which also helped train AI for self-driving cars. As artificial intelligence became able to solve many CAPTCHAs, newer systems analyze mouse movements, browsing behavior and device information invisibly, often asking users only to tick a box saying ‘I’m not a robot’.",
s:[["The Turing test","The name refers to the Turing test, proposed by Alan Turing in 1950 to judge whether a machine can imitate human conversation. A CAPTCHA is a reverse Turing test, administered by a computer to judge whether the user is human."],["Digitizing books","The original reCAPTCHA showed two words: one the computer knew, to check the user, and one from a scanned book that optical character recognition had failed to read. By combining many users’ answers, it helped digitize the archives of The New York Times and millions of Google Books."],["Accessibility","Visual CAPTCHAs can be difficult for people who are blind or have low vision, dyslexia or other disabilities. Audio CAPTCHAs offer an alternative but are often hard to understand. Accessibility advocates push for less burdensome methods."],["Bots fight back","Criminals sometimes pay people in low-wage ‘CAPTCHA farms’ to solve challenges, and modern AI can read distorted text and identify images better than many humans. This has pushed websites toward invisible risk scoring and passkeys."]],
k:[["Stands for","Completely Automated Public Turing test to tell Computers and Humans Apart"],["Coined","2003, Carnegie Mellon University"],["reCAPTCHA","2007 (bought by Google in 2009)"],["Purpose","Block bots"],["Modern approach","Invisible behavior analysis"]],
d:[["1950","Alan Turing proposes the Turing test"],["1997","Early text-distortion test used by AltaVista"],["2003","The term CAPTCHA is coined"],["2007","reCAPTCHA launched"],["2009","Google acquires reCAPTCHA"],["2014","‘I’m not a robot’ checkbox introduced"]],
s2:[["Luis von Ahn","Luis von Ahn, a Guatemalan-born computer scientist, also co-founded the language-learning app Duolingo. He has said he was inspired by the idea of using the time millions of people spent solving CAPTCHAs for something useful."],["Annoying but useful","Studies estimated that people worldwide spent hundreds of thousands of hours a day solving CAPTCHAs. Researchers continue to look for ways to keep out bots without wasting users’ time."]],
sp:["‘I’m not a robot’","The simple checkbox introduced in 2014 works by analyzing how the user moves the mouse and interacts with the page before clicking. Bots tend to move in perfectly straight lines or too quickly."],
g:[["Bot","An automated software program"],["Turing test","A test of whether a machine can seem human"],["Optical character recognition","Software that reads printed text from images"],["Accessibility","Ease of use for people with disabilities"]],
f:["CAPTCHA was coined in 2003.","reCAPTCHA helped digitize old books.","Clicking traffic lights helped train AI."],
fun:"By clicking on images of traffic lights and buses, millions of people unknowingly helped train artificial intelligence systems for self-driving cars."});

ADDART({id:"laptops",t:"Laptops and Tablets",c:"Technology",i:"💻",w:"Laptop",r:["comp","smartphone","touchscreens","battery","operatingsystems"],
p:[["Computers on the go","Laptops and tablets are portable computers with built-in screens and batteries."],["From luggables to ultrabooks","Early portables weighed more than 10 kilograms."],["Tablets with touch","The iPad made touchscreen tablets popular in 2010."]],
l:"Laptops, also called notebook computers, are portable personal computers that combine a screen, keyboard, pointing device and battery in one folding case. Tablets are flat, touchscreen computers without built-in keyboards. Early portable computers, such as the Osborne 1 of 1981, weighed about 11 kilograms and were called ‘luggables’. The clamshell design, with a screen folding over the keyboard, appeared in the GRiD Compass of 1982, which was used by NASA on Space Shuttle missions. Through the 1990s, laptops became lighter and more powerful, adding color screens, trackpads and wireless networking. By 2008, laptops outsold desktop computers in the United States. Apple’s MacBook Air (2008) popularized very thin laptops, and inexpensive Chromebooks became common in schools. Earlier tablets and handheld computers existed, such as the Apple Newton and Microsoft Tablet PC, but Apple’s iPad, released in April 2010, made tablets a mass market. Today, laptops are the main computers for many students and workers, while tablets are used for reading, video, drawing and education, and many devices convert between the two.",
s:[["Early portables","The Osborne 1 had a 5-inch screen and no battery; it had to be plugged in. The Compaq Portable of 1983 was nicknamed a ‘luggable’. The Epson HX-20 of 1981, about the size of a sheet of paper, is often called the first true laptop, running on rechargeable batteries."],["Key technologies","Laptops depended on improvements in flat LCD screens, rechargeable lithium-ion batteries, low-power processors and, later, solid-state drives. Wi-Fi, built into most laptops from the early 2000s, made them truly mobile."],["The iPad","Steve Jobs introduced the iPad in January 2010. Apple sold 3 million in its first 80 days. Tablets became popular with children, older people and businesses, and drawing tablets with styluses are used by artists and designers."],["Laptops in education","Projects such as One Laptop per Child, launched in 2005, aimed to provide low-cost laptops to children in developing countries. During the COVID-19 pandemic, laptops and tablets became essential for remote learning, though many students lacked access."]],
k:[["First portable computer","Osborne 1, 1981"],["First clamshell laptop","GRiD Compass, 1982"],["Laptops outsold desktops (US)","2008"],["iPad released","April 2010"],["Key battery","Lithium-ion"]],
d:[["1981","Osborne 1 and Epson HX-20 released"],["1982","GRiD Compass clamshell laptop"],["1991","Apple PowerBook introduces a palm rest and trackball"],["2005","One Laptop per Child project announced"],["2008","MacBook Air released"],["2010","Apple iPad released"]],
s2:[["Alan Kay’s Dynabook","In 1972, computer scientist Alan Kay described the Dynabook, a book-sized personal computer for children with a flat screen and wireless connection, decades before laptops and tablets made the idea real."],["Two-in-ones","Many modern devices combine laptop and tablet, with detachable keyboards or screens that fold back. Microsoft’s Surface line, launched in 2012, popularized this design."]],
sp:["Laptops in space","The GRiD Compass flew on the Space Shuttle in 1983. Today, astronauts on the International Space Station use dozens of laptops to control experiments, check procedures and talk with family."],
g:[["Laptop","A portable folding computer"],["Tablet","A flat touchscreen computer"],["Clamshell","A design with a hinged lid"],["Trackpad","A touch-sensitive pointing surface"]],
f:["The Osborne 1 weighed about 11 kilograms.","Laptops outsold desktops in the US in 2008.","The iPad was released in 2010."],
fun:"The first laptop to go to space, the GRiD Compass, cost about 8,000 dollars in 1982."});

ADDART({id:"arcadegames",t:"Arcade Games and Pinball",c:"Technology",i:"🕹️",w:"Arcade video game",r:["videogames","esports","toys","japancountry","comp","emulators","pixelart"],
p:[["Coin-operated fun","Arcade games are coin-operated machines in public places."],["Pong and Pac-Man","Arcade hits launched the video game industry."],["Pinball’s comeback","Pinball, once banned in some cities, has enjoyed a revival."]],
l:"Arcade games are coin-operated entertainment machines found in amusement arcades, bars, restaurants and malls, including video games, pinball machines and redemption games. Pinball developed from the 18th-century table game bagatelle; coin-operated pinball machines spread in the 1930s, and flippers were added in 1947. In the 1940s, some US cities, including New York, banned pinball as a form of gambling; New York’s ban lasted until 1976. Video arcade games began with Computer Space in 1971 and Atari’s Pong in 1972, a simple table-tennis game that became a huge hit. The ‘golden age’ of arcade video games followed in the late 1970s and early 1980s, with Space Invaders (1978), Asteroids (1979), Pac-Man (1980) and Donkey Kong (1981), which introduced the character later called Mario. Fighting games such as Street Fighter II (1991) revived arcades in the 1990s. Home consoles and computers eventually reduced arcade attendance in many countries, but arcades remain popular in Japan, and ‘barcades’ and retro gaming have brought them back elsewhere.",
s:[["Pinball","A pinball machine uses flippers, bumpers and ramps to keep a steel ball in play, scoring points. Mechanical machines gave way to electronic ones in the 1970s. In 1976, Roger Sharpe convinced the New York City Council that pinball was a game of skill by calling a shot and making it, helping overturn the ban."],["The golden age","Space Invaders was so popular in Japan that it was said to cause a shortage of 100-yen coins, though this may be a myth. Pac-Man, designed by Toru Iwatani, became one of the highest-grossing video games ever and appealed to people who had never played games before."],["High scores","Arcade games recorded the best players’ initials, inspiring competition. The 2007 documentary The King of Kong followed players competing for the Donkey Kong record. Competitive gaming in arcades was a forerunner of e-sports."],["Arcades in Japan","Japanese game centers remain popular, with rhythm games, crane games, photo booths called purikura and large multiplayer cabinets. Many are in busy entertainment districts such as Akihabara in Tokyo."]],
k:[["Pinball flippers added","1947"],["First commercial video arcade game","Computer Space, 1971"],["Breakthrough hit","Pong, 1972"],["Golden age","Late 1970s–early 1980s"],["New York pinball ban","1942–1976"]],
d:[["1931","Coin-operated pinball spreads"],["1942","New York City bans pinball"],["1947","Flippers added to pinball"],["1972","Atari’s Pong released"],["1978","Space Invaders released"],["1980","Pac-Man released"],["1991","Street Fighter II revives arcades"]],
s2:[["Pac-Man’s design","Toru Iwatani said Pac-Man’s shape was inspired by a pizza with a slice missing. Each of the four ghosts has its own pattern of chasing Pac-Man, giving the game strategy."],["Donkey Kong","Nintendo’s Donkey Kong (1981), designed by Shigeru Miyamoto, introduced a carpenter called Jumpman, later renamed Mario, who became the most famous video game character."]],
sp:["Pong’s first test","Atari placed its first Pong machine in Andy Capp’s Tavern in Sunnyvale, California, in 1972. Days later the bar owner called to say it had broken; it was so full of coins that the coin box had jammed."],
g:[["Arcade","A place with coin-operated games"],["Pinball","A game of keeping a ball in play with flippers"],["Cabinet","The box housing an arcade game"],["High score","The best score recorded on a game"]],
f:["Pinball was banned in New York until 1976.","Pong was released in 1972.","Mario first appeared in Donkey Kong."],
fun:"The first Pong machine stopped working because so many coins were stuffed into it that the coin box overflowed."});

ADDART({id:"rubikscube",t:"The Rubik’s Cube",c:"Technology",i:"🧊",w:"Rubik's Cube",r:["toys","alg","robot","boardgames","esports"],
p:[["A famous puzzle","The Rubik’s Cube is a colorful 3D puzzle invented in Hungary in 1974."],["Quintillions of positions","It has about 43 quintillion possible arrangements."],["Speedcubing","The fastest solvers finish in under three seconds."]],
l:"The Rubik’s Cube is a three-dimensional combination puzzle invented in 1974 by Hungarian architecture professor Ernő Rubik, who created it to help explain three-dimensional movement to his students. The classic cube has six faces, each with nine colored squares; each face can be turned, mixing up the colors, and the goal is to return each face to a single color. Rubik needed a month to solve his own cube for the first time. Launched internationally in 1980, it became a worldwide craze, selling about 100 million cubes in its first few years, and it remains one of the best-selling toys ever, with more than 500 million sold. The standard cube has about 43 quintillion (43,252,003,274,489,856,000) possible arrangements, yet in 2010 mathematicians and computer scientists proved that any position can be solved in 20 moves or fewer, a number nicknamed ‘God’s number’. Speedcubing competitions are held worldwide under the World Cube Association, and in 2026 a solver in Poland recorded the first official solve under three seconds. Variations include larger cubes and other shapes.",
s:[["How it works","A clever internal mechanism lets the pieces turn without falling apart. The cube has 26 visible pieces: 6 centers that stay fixed relative to each other, 8 corners and 12 edges. Solvers learn sequences of moves called algorithms to move pieces without disturbing others."],["Speedcubing","Speedcubers use special lubricated cubes and memorize many algorithms. Popular methods solve the cube in layers. Competitions include events such as one-handed solving, blindfolded solving after memorizing the cube, and larger cubes up to 7×7×7."],["God’s number","Researchers used about 35 years of computer processing time, donated by Google, to show in 2010 that every position can be solved in at most 20 moves. Finding the shortest solution for a given position is still computationally difficult."],["Cubes and robots","Robots have solved Rubik’s Cubes in a fraction of a second; in 2024, a Japanese robot solved one in about 0.3 seconds. In 2019, an AI system learned to solve the cube without human knowledge."]],
k:[["Inventor","Ernő Rubik, 1974"],["Country","Hungary"],["Possible positions","About 43 quintillion"],["God’s number","20 moves"],["Cubes sold","More than 500 million"]],
d:[["1974","Ernő Rubik invents the cube"],["1977","Cube first sold in Hungary"],["1980","Cube launched internationally"],["1982","First world championship in Budapest"],["2010","God’s number proved to be 20"],["2026","First official solve under three seconds"]],
s2:[["The 1980s craze","At the height of the craze, books explaining how to solve the cube topped bestseller lists, and there was even a cartoon series, Rubik, the Amazing Cube. Many people peeled off and rearranged the stickers in frustration."],["Ernő Rubik","Rubik did not originally intend to make a toy, calling his invention the ‘Magic Cube’. He became one of the first self-made millionaires in communist Hungary."]],
sp:["Blindfolded solving","In blindfolded speedcubing, solvers inspect the cube, memorize its pattern, put on a blindfold and then solve it. Top competitors complete the whole process in less than 15 seconds."],
g:[["Algorithm","A sequence of moves with a specific result"],["Speedcubing","Solving cubes as fast as possible"],["God’s number","The maximum moves needed to solve any position"],["Combination puzzle","A puzzle solved by arranging pieces"]],
f:["The Rubik’s Cube was invented in 1974.","Any position can be solved in 20 moves or fewer.","The first official sub-3-second solve came in 2026."],
fun:"If you turned a Rubik’s Cube once every second, it would take you about 1.4 trillion years to go through all of its positions."});

ADDART({id:"lego",t:"LEGO",c:"Technology",i:"🧱",w:"Lego",r:["toys","plastics","printing3d","robot","boardgames"],
p:[["Building bricks","LEGO bricks are interlocking plastic toys from Denmark."],["Still compatible","Bricks made today still fit bricks made in 1958."],["More than toys","LEGO is used in education, robotics and art."]],
l:"LEGO is a line of plastic construction toys made by the Danish company the LEGO Group, best known for its interlocking bricks that can be assembled and taken apart to build almost anything. The company was founded in 1932 in Billund, Denmark, by carpenter Ole Kirk Christiansen, who made wooden toys; the name comes from the Danish words leg godt, meaning ‘play well’. The company began making plastic bricks in 1949, and in 1958 it patented the modern brick design with hollow tubes inside, which gives bricks their strong ‘clutch power’. Bricks made today are still compatible with those made in 1958. LEGO introduced the minifigure in 1978 and themed sets such as space, castles and pirates, and later licensed sets based on films such as Star Wars, which began in 1999. LEGO Mindstorms (1998) brought programmable robots to classrooms, and LEGO competitions teach engineering. LEGO bricks are made to tolerances as fine as a few thousandths of a millimeter. The LEGO Group became the world’s largest toy company by revenue and is working to make bricks from more sustainable materials.",
s:[["The brick","A standard 2×4 brick has eight studs on top and three tubes underneath. Six 2×4 bricks of the same color can be combined in more than 915 million ways. Bricks are made from ABS plastic in molds so precise that only about 18 in a million are rejected."],["Minifigures","The LEGO minifigure, introduced in 1978, has movable arms, legs and head. Billions have been made, and minifigures are sometimes said to outnumber the human population."],["LEGO in education","LEGO Education and the FIRST LEGO League competitions teach children engineering, programming and teamwork using robots built from LEGO parts. Researchers have also used LEGO to build prototypes and scientific instruments."],["Sustainability","Most LEGO bricks are made from oil-based plastic. The company has made some pieces, such as trees and leaves, from plant-based plastic from sugarcane since 2018 and continues to search for sustainable materials that keep the same quality."]],
k:[["Founded","1932, Billund, Denmark"],["Name meaning","Leg godt, ‘play well’"],["Modern brick patented","1958"],["Minifigure introduced","1978"],["Combinations of six 2×4 bricks","More than 915 million"]],
d:[["1932","Ole Kirk Christiansen starts making wooden toys"],["1949","First plastic bricks"],["1958","Modern LEGO brick patented"],["1968","Legoland Billund opens"],["1978","Minifigure introduced"],["1998","LEGO Mindstorms launched"],["2014","The LEGO Movie released"]],
s2:[["Legoland","The first Legoland theme park opened in Billund in 1968. Legoland parks now operate in several countries, featuring huge models of cities, landmarks and characters built from millions of bricks."],["The LEGO Movie","The LEGO Movie (2014), with its song ‘Everything Is Awesome’, was a box-office hit and led to sequels and spin-offs, introducing LEGO to new generations."]],
sp:["Stepping on LEGO","Stepping on a LEGO brick in bare feet is famously painful because bricks are hard and distribute weight onto small points. The company once made slippers designed to protect feet from bricks."],
g:[["Stud","A bump on top of a brick"],["Clutch power","How firmly bricks hold together"],["Minifigure","A small LEGO person"],["ABS plastic","A tough plastic used for bricks"]],
f:["LEGO comes from Danish words meaning ‘play well’.","Bricks from 1958 still fit today’s bricks.","Six 2×4 bricks can combine in over 915 million ways."],
fun:"A tower of about 375,000 LEGO bricks would be needed before the bottom brick broke under the weight, according to a test by scientists."});

ADDART({id:"pens",t:"Pens and Pencils",c:"Technology",i:"✏️",w:"Pen",r:["writing","paper","print","typewriter","calligraphy"],
p:[["Writing tools","Pens and pencils are among the most common tools ever made."],["The ballpoint revolution","The ballpoint pen made writing cheap and easy."],["Pencils use graphite","‘Lead’ pencils actually contain graphite and clay."]],
l:"Pens and pencils are handheld tools for writing and drawing. Ancient Egyptians wrote with reed pens dipped in ink, and in medieval Europe people used quill pens made from bird feathers. Steel dip pens became common in the 19th century, and fountain pens with internal ink reservoirs, perfected by inventors such as Lewis Waterman in 1884, became popular. The ballpoint pen, which uses a tiny rotating ball to roll quick-drying ink onto paper, was patented by Hungarian journalist László Bíró in 1938; mass production after World War II, notably by Marcel Bich’s BIC company from 1950, made pens cheap enough for everyone. Felt-tip pens and markers spread in the 1960s, and gel pens in the 1980s. Pencils use a core of graphite, a form of carbon, mixed with clay; a large deposit of pure graphite discovered in Borrowdale, England, in the 16th century started the pencil industry. The core is called ‘lead’ only because graphite was once mistaken for lead ore. Pencil hardness is graded from hard (H) to soft and black (B). Despite keyboards and touchscreens, billions of pens and pencils are still made each year.",
s:[["The ballpoint","László Bíró noticed that newspaper ink dried quickly and designed a pen with a ball bearing in its tip to spread thick ink. He fled to Argentina during World War II, where the pen was produced; in Argentina and some other countries, ballpoints are still called ‘biromes’ or ‘biros’. The BIC Cristal, introduced in 1950, has sold more than 100 billion units."],["The pencil","Wooden pencils hold a core glued between two grooved strips of wood. In 1795, French scientist Nicolas-Jacques Conté developed a method of mixing graphite with clay and baking it, allowing pencils of different hardness. The common US school pencil, the No. 2, is equivalent to HB."],["Erasers","Rubber was named in 1770 by English scientist Joseph Priestley, who noticed it could rub out pencil marks. Hymen Lipman patented a pencil with an attached eraser in 1858."],["Pens in space","A popular story says NASA spent millions developing a space pen while the Soviets used pencils. In fact, both used pencils at first, but pencil tips and graphite dust are hazards in spacecraft. The Fisher Space Pen, developed privately with a pressurized ink cartridge, was bought by both NASA and the Soviet program."]],
k:[["Ballpoint inventor","László Bíró, 1938"],["Fountain pen improver","Lewis Waterman, 1884"],["Pencil core","Graphite and clay"],["Graphite deposit","Borrowdale, England (1500s)"],["Pencil grades","From H (hard) to B (soft)"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BC","Egyptians write with reed pens"],["c. 600","Quill pens come into use"],["1565","Graphite pencil described by Conrad Gessner"],["1795","Conté invents the modern pencil lead process"],["1884","Waterman’s fountain pen patented"],["1938","Bíró patents the ballpoint pen"],["1950","BIC Cristal introduced"]],
s2:[["How long can a pencil write?","A typical pencil can draw a line about 56 kilometers long or write about 45,000 words, according to popular estimates."],["Pens and history","Many famous documents were signed with dip pens or quills, including the US Declaration of Independence. Some national leaders still sign laws with several pens and give them away as souvenirs."]],
sp:["No lead in pencils","Pencils have never contained lead. Early users thought the newly discovered graphite was a type of lead ore and called it ‘plumbago’, meaning lead-like, and the name stuck."],
g:[["Graphite","A soft form of carbon"],["Ballpoint","A pen that writes with a rolling ball"],["Fountain pen","A pen with an internal ink reservoir"],["Quill","A pen made from a feather"]],
f:["Pencils contain graphite, not lead.","The ballpoint pen was patented in 1938.","The BIC Cristal has sold over 100 billion units."],
fun:"The BIC Cristal pen is one of the best-selling consumer products in history, with more than 100 billion sold."});

ADDART({id:"eyeglasses",t:"Eyeglasses and Contact Lenses",c:"Health and the Body",i:"👓",w:"Glasses",r:["eye","light","glass","illusions","scope","refraction"],
p:[["Seeing clearly","Eyeglasses and contact lenses correct blurry vision."],["Medieval invention","Eyeglasses were invented in Italy around 1286."],["Billions need them","Billions of people need glasses, but many cannot get them."]],
l:"Eyeglasses, or spectacles, are frames holding lenses in front of the eyes to correct vision problems, while contact lenses sit directly on the eye’s surface. The most common problems are nearsightedness (myopia), in which distant objects look blurry; farsightedness (hyperopia); astigmatism; and presbyopia, the age-related loss of near focus that makes reading difficult after about age 40. Eyeglasses were invented in northern Italy around 1286, probably by glassmakers near Pisa or Venice; early versions were convex lenses for reading held on the nose. Concave lenses for nearsightedness followed in the 15th century, and Benjamin Franklin invented bifocals in the 1780s. Contact lenses were first made of glass in the 1880s; soft hydrogel lenses, introduced in 1971, made them comfortable and popular. Today, laser eye surgery can also reshape the cornea. The World Health Organization estimates that at least a billion people have vision impairment that could have been prevented or corrected, often because they lack access to glasses. Myopia is becoming much more common worldwide, especially among children in East Asia.",
s:[["How lenses work","A nearsighted eye focuses light in front of the retina, so concave lenses spread light slightly before it enters the eye. Farsighted eyes focus behind the retina, corrected by convex lenses. Lens strength is measured in diopters."],["The myopia boom","In some East Asian cities, more than 80 percent of young adults are nearsighted. Researchers think spending more time indoors and doing close work contributes; studies show that more time outdoors in daylight reduces children’s risk of developing myopia."],["Contact lenses","Contact lenses float on the tear film covering the cornea. Rigid gas-permeable lenses let oxygen through, while soft lenses are comfortable and may be disposable daily. Poor hygiene can cause serious eye infections, so users must follow cleaning instructions."],["Access to glasses","Simple reading glasses can cost very little, yet hundreds of millions of people lack them, affecting education and work. Programs train local workers to test eyes and supply affordable glasses."]],
k:[["Invented","Italy, about 1286"],["Bifocals","Benjamin Franklin, 1780s"],["Soft contact lenses","1971"],["Most common problem","Myopia (nearsightedness)"],["People with preventable vision impairment","At least 1 billion (WHO)"]],
d:[["c. 1286","Eyeglasses invented in Italy"],["1400s","Concave lenses for nearsightedness"],["1780s","Franklin invents bifocals"],["1888","First glass contact lenses"],["1971","Soft contact lenses approved in the US"],["1990s","Laser eye surgery becomes widespread"]],
s2:[["A sermon about glasses","In 1306, Friar Giordano da Pisa said in a sermon in Florence that eyeglasses had been invented ‘not yet twenty years’ earlier, the main evidence for their invention around 1286."],["Sunglasses","Inuit peoples carved snow goggles with narrow slits to reduce glare long before sunglasses. Modern sunglasses spread in the 1930s, and good ones block harmful ultraviolet light."]],
sp:["Glasses in art","One of the earliest paintings showing eyeglasses is a 1352 fresco by Tommaso da Modena in Treviso, Italy, depicting a cardinal reading with spectacles."],
g:[["Myopia","Nearsightedness"],["Presbyopia","Age-related loss of near focus"],["Diopter","A unit of lens strength"],["Cornea","The clear front surface of the eye"]],
f:["Eyeglasses were invented around 1286.","Benjamin Franklin invented bifocals.","More time outdoors may reduce myopia in children."],
fun:"Early eyeglasses had no arms; people balanced them on their noses or held them up, and arms that rested on the ears became common only in the 18th century."});

ADDART({id:"firesafety",t:"Fire Extinguishers and Sprinklers",c:"Technology",i:"🧯",w:"Fire extinguisher",r:["firefighters","smokedetectors","thermo","reactions","skyscrapers"],
p:[["Fighting fires early","Fire extinguishers and sprinklers put out fires before they spread."],["Different fires, different extinguishers","Water, foam, powder and carbon dioxide suit different fires."],["Sprinklers save lives","Automatic sprinklers greatly reduce deaths in building fires."]],
l:"Fire extinguishers and automatic sprinkler systems are devices for putting out or controlling fires in their early stages. Fire needs heat, fuel and oxygen, known as the fire triangle, and extinguishers work by removing one or more of these, cooling the fire, smothering it or interrupting the chemical reaction. The first portable pressurized fire extinguisher was invented by British army officer George William Manby in 1818, using a copper vessel of potassium carbonate solution and compressed air. Today’s extinguishers use water, foam, dry chemical powder, carbon dioxide or wet chemicals, each suited to particular kinds of fire; using water on burning oil or electrical equipment can be dangerous. Automatic fire sprinklers, developed in the 19th century and perfected by Henry Parmelee in 1874 and Frederick Grinnell, spray water when heat breaks a glass bulb or melts a link in a sprinkler head, usually only above the fire. Studies by the US National Fire Protection Association show sprinklers sharply reduce deaths and damage in building fires. Building codes require sprinklers in many new buildings.",
s:[["Classes of fire","Fires are classified by fuel: ordinary materials such as wood and paper, flammable liquids, gases, electrical equipment, metals and cooking oils. Labels on extinguishers show which types they can fight. Multipurpose dry powder extinguishers work on several classes."],["Using an extinguisher","Many fire services teach the PASS method: Pull the pin, Aim at the base of the fire, Squeeze the handle, Sweep from side to side. People should use extinguishers only on small fires and leave immediately if a fire grows."],["How sprinklers work","Each sprinkler head has a heat-sensitive glass bulb filled with liquid that expands and shatters at about 68°C, or a metal link that melts. Only heads near the fire activate, contrary to the movie image of every sprinkler going off at once."],["Fire blankets and kitchens","Kitchen fires, often from cooking oil, should never be put out with water, which can cause an explosive fireball. Fire blankets and wet chemical extinguishers smother the fire. Turning off the heat and covering the pan with a lid can also help."]],
k:[["Fire triangle","Heat, fuel and oxygen"],["First portable extinguisher","George Manby, 1818"],["Automatic sprinkler","Henry Parmelee, 1874"],["Common extinguishing agents","Water, foam, powder, carbon dioxide"],["Sprinkler trigger","Heat-sensitive bulb or link"]],
d:[["1723","Ambrose Godfrey patents an early fire extinguisher"],["1818","Manby invents the portable pressurized extinguisher"],["1874","Parmelee patents an automatic sprinkler"],["1881","Grinnell improves the sprinkler head"],["1911","Triangle Shirtwaist Factory fire leads to safety reforms"]],
s2:[["The Triangle fire","On 25 March 1911, a fire at the Triangle Shirtwaist Factory in New York killed 146 garment workers, mostly young immigrant women, many trapped by locked doors. The disaster led to new laws requiring sprinklers, fire drills and better exits."],["Halon","Halon gas was once widely used to fight fires in computer rooms and aircraft because it leaves no residue. It was phased out under the Montreal Protocol because it damages the ozone layer, though some aircraft still use it."]],
sp:["Fire safety at home","Fire services recommend keeping a small extinguisher and fire blanket in the kitchen, installing smoke alarms and planning escape routes. In a fire, people should get out, stay out and call emergency services."],
g:[["Fire triangle","Heat, fuel and oxygen needed for fire"],["Extinguisher","A device that puts out fires"],["Sprinkler","A device that sprays water when heated"],["Smother","To cut off a fire’s oxygen"]],
f:["Fire needs heat, fuel and oxygen.","Only sprinklers near a fire go off.","Never put water on an oil fire."],
fun:"In films, every sprinkler in a building often goes off at once, but in reality each sprinkler head activates only when heat reaches it."});

ADDART({id:"carsafety",t:"Seat Belts and Airbags",c:"Technology",i:"🚗",w:"Automobile safety",r:["cars","trafficlights","selfdriving","roads","newton"],
p:[["Saving lives on the road","Seat belts and airbags protect people in car crashes."],["The three-point belt","Volvo engineer Nils Bohlin invented the modern seat belt in 1959."],["Fewer road deaths","Safety features have made cars far safer than in the past."]],
l:"Car safety features protect people in vehicles from injury during crashes. The most important is the seat belt: the modern three-point seat belt, crossing the lap and chest, was invented by Swedish engineer Nils Bohlin at Volvo in 1959, and Volvo made its patent freely available so that all carmakers could use it. Seat belts reduce the risk of death for front-seat passengers by about 45 percent in cars. Airbags, which inflate in a fraction of a second during a crash, were developed in the 1950s to 1970s and became common in the 1990s; they work together with seat belts and are not a replacement. Other features include crumple zones that absorb crash energy, safety glass, child car seats, anti-lock brakes and electronic stability control. Newer cars have automatic emergency braking, lane-keeping assistance and blind-spot warnings. Road safety campaigns, laws requiring seat belts and crash tests have greatly reduced deaths in many countries, yet the World Health Organization estimates that about 1.2 million people still die in road crashes each year, most in low- and middle-income countries.",
s:[["How seat belts work","In a crash, a car stops suddenly, but unbelted occupants keep moving until they hit the dashboard, windshield or other passengers. Seat belts spread the force across the strong bones of the pelvis and chest. Pretensioners tighten belts instantly in a crash, and load limiters prevent excessive force."],["Airbags","Sensors detect a crash and trigger a chemical reaction that fills the airbag with gas in about 30 milliseconds. The bag then deflates as the occupant presses into it, cushioning the impact. Faulty airbag inflators made by the Japanese company Takata led to the largest automotive recall in history from 2013."],["Crash tests","Organizations such as Euro NCAP and the US Insurance Institute for Highway Safety crash cars into barriers using instrumented dummies and rate their safety. Crash test dummies have mostly been based on average male bodies; newer dummies represent women and children more accurately."],["Ralph Nader","American lawyer Ralph Nader’s 1965 book Unsafe at Any Speed criticized carmakers for neglecting safety. It helped lead to the creation of the US National Highway Traffic Safety Administration in 1970 and mandatory safety standards."]],
k:[["Three-point seat belt","Nils Bohlin, Volvo, 1959"],["Risk reduction from seat belts","About 45% for front-seat occupants"],["Airbags common","1990s"],["Airbag inflation time","About 30 milliseconds"],["Global road deaths","About 1.2 million a year (WHO)"]],
d:[["1959","Volvo introduces the three-point seat belt"],["1965","Unsafe at Any Speed published"],["1970","Australia’s state of Victoria makes seat belts compulsory"],["1973","First cars with airbags sold in the US"],["1983","UK makes front seat belts compulsory"],["2013","Takata airbag recalls begin"]],
s2:[["Volvo’s gift","Volvo kept its patent on the three-point belt open for any carmaker to use, saying it was too important to keep to itself. Experts estimate it has saved more than a million lives."],["Self-driving cars and safety","Most crashes involve human error, such as distraction, speeding or drunk driving. Supporters of self-driving cars argue they could prevent many crashes, though the technology has its own risks."]],
sp:["Crash test dummies","The first crash test dummy, Sierra Sam, was built in 1949 to test aircraft ejection seats. Modern dummies contain dozens of sensors measuring forces on the head, neck, chest and legs."],
g:[["Three-point belt","A seat belt crossing the lap and chest"],["Crumple zone","A part of a car designed to absorb crash energy"],["Airbag","A cushion that inflates in a crash"],["Recall","When a maker asks owners to return products for repair"]],
f:["The three-point seat belt was invented in 1959.","Airbags inflate in about 30 milliseconds.","About 1.2 million people die in road crashes each year."],
fun:"Volvo gave away the patent for its life-saving three-point seat belt so that every carmaker could use it."});

ADDART({id:"creditcards",t:"Credit and Debit Cards",c:"Technology",i:"💳",w:"Payment card",r:["money","digitalpay","rfid","atms","cybersecurity"],
p:[["Plastic money","Credit and debit cards let people pay without cash."],["Born in the 1950s","Diners Club launched the first general charge card in 1950."],["Chips and taps","Cards now use chips and contactless payment."]],
l:"Credit and debit cards are plastic or metal cards that let people pay for goods and services without cash. A credit card lets the holder borrow money from a bank up to a limit and pay it back later, usually with interest if the balance is not paid in full; a debit card takes money directly from the holder’s bank account. Store credit plates and charge cards existed in the early 20th century, but the first general-purpose charge card, Diners Club, was launched in 1950 after businessman Frank McNamara reportedly forgot his wallet at a restaurant. American Express followed in 1958, and BankAmericard, later Visa, began in 1958, letting customers carry a balance. Magnetic stripes were added in the 1960s and 1970s, and chips (EMV), which make cards harder to copy, spread from the 1990s. Contactless cards, using NFC radio technology, let people pay with a tap, and phone wallets now often replace physical cards. Visa and Mastercard run the largest networks. Card payments brought convenience but also debt, fees and fraud, and many people worldwide still lack bank accounts.",
s:[["How a card payment works","When a card is used, the shop’s terminal sends payment details through the card network to the cardholder’s bank, which approves or declines the payment within seconds. The shop pays a fee on each transaction, which is part of how card companies make money."],["Chip and PIN","Chips generate a unique code for each transaction, making cloned cards much less useful. Many countries combine chips with a PIN. The United States adopted chip cards widely only around 2015, later than Europe."],["Debt","Credit cards can help people manage spending and build credit histories but can also lead to debt because of high interest rates. Financial advisers recommend paying the full balance each month when possible."],["Fraud","Criminals steal card details using skimmers, data breaches and phishing. Banks use fraud detection systems that look for unusual spending and can block suspicious payments. Customers are usually protected from liability for fraud they report quickly."]],
k:[["First general charge card","Diners Club, 1950"],["BankAmericard (Visa)","1958"],["Security chip","EMV chip"],["Contactless technology","NFC"],["Largest networks","Visa and Mastercard"]],
d:[["1950","Diners Club card launched"],["1958","American Express card and BankAmericard launched"],["1966","Interbank, later Mastercard, formed"],["1970s","Magnetic stripes become standard"],["1990s","Chip cards introduced in Europe"],["2007","Contactless cards spread"]],
s2:[["The ‘first supper’","According to Diners Club, Frank McNamara forgot his wallet at a New York restaurant in 1949 and paid with a card the following year, an event the company called the ‘First Supper’. Some historians consider the story partly marketing legend."],["Card sizes","Payment cards follow an international standard size, 85.6 by 54 millimeters, also used for driver’s licenses and ID cards in many countries."]],
sp:["The magnetic stripe","IBM engineer Forrest Parry developed the magnetic stripe card in the 1960s. He reportedly got the idea when his wife suggested using a hot iron to attach a strip of magnetic tape to a plastic card."],
g:[["Credit card","A card for borrowing money to pay"],["Debit card","A card that takes money directly from a bank account"],["Interest","The cost of borrowing money"],["EMV chip","A security chip in payment cards"]],
f:["Diners Club was the first general charge card.","Chip cards are harder to copy than magnetic stripes.","Contactless cards use NFC."],
fun:"The magnetic stripe on cards was attached using a household iron in its inventor’s first experiments."});

ADDART({id:"machinetools",t:"Machine Tools",c:"Technology",i:"⚙️",w:"Machine tool",r:["indrev","steel","printing3d","robot","metals"],
p:[["Machines that make machines","Machine tools cut and shape metal parts precisely."],["Lathes and mills","Lathes spin the workpiece; milling machines spin the cutter."],["Computer control","CNC machines are guided by computer programs."]],
l:"Machine tools are powered machines used to cut, drill, grind and shape metal and other rigid materials into precise parts, making them the foundation of manufacturing; they are sometimes called the ‘machines that make machines’. The most important types are the lathe, which spins a workpiece against a cutting tool to make round parts such as shafts and screws, and the milling machine, which uses a rotating cutter to shape flat and complex surfaces. Others include drill presses, grinders and saws. Simple lathes existed in ancient Egypt, but precision machine tools emerged during the Industrial Revolution: John Wilkinson’s boring machine of 1775 allowed accurate cylinders for James Watt’s steam engines, and Henry Maudslay’s screw-cutting lathe of about 1800 made standardized screws possible. Interchangeable parts, produced by machine tools, enabled mass production of guns, sewing machines, bicycles and cars. Since the 1950s, numerical control, and later computer numerical control (CNC), has let computers guide machine tools with extreme precision. Today, CNC machining works alongside 3D printing and robotics in factories worldwide.",
s:[["The lathe","On a lathe, the workpiece spins while a sharp tool removes material. Woodworkers use lathes to make bowls and table legs; metal lathes make precise cylindrical parts. Henry Maudslay’s lathe used a lead screw to move the cutting tool precisely, allowing identical screw threads."],["Precision and standards","Machine tools allowed parts to be made to precise measurements, so that parts from one machine fit another. British engineer Joseph Whitworth standardized screw threads in 1841, and precise measuring tools such as micrometers developed alongside machine tools."],["CNC","Numerical control was developed in the late 1940s and 1950s, partly by John T. Parsons and MIT, to make complex aircraft parts. CNC machines read instructions called G-code from computer-aided design files and can cut parts accurate to a few thousandths of a millimeter."],["Subtractive vs additive","Machine tools are ‘subtractive’, removing material to make parts, while 3D printers are ‘additive’, building parts layer by layer. Many factories combine both, printing complex shapes and finishing them on CNC machines."]],
k:[["Main types","Lathes, milling machines, drills, grinders"],["Boring machine","John Wilkinson, 1775"],["Screw-cutting lathe","Henry Maudslay, about 1800"],["Standard screw threads","Joseph Whitworth, 1841"],["Computer control","CNC"]],
d:[["c. 1300 BC","Lathes used in ancient Egypt"],["1775","Wilkinson’s boring machine"],["c. 1800","Maudslay’s screw-cutting lathe"],["1818","Eli Whitney credited with an early milling machine"],["1841","Whitworth standard screw threads"],["1952","First numerically controlled milling machine at MIT"]],
s2:[["Interchangeable parts","The idea of interchangeable parts, demonstrated with muskets in France and the United States around 1800, was called the ‘American system of manufacturing’. It depended on machine tools and gauges and made mass production possible."],["The importance of machine tools","Countries consider machine tools strategically important because they are needed to make weapons, aircraft and other machines. China, Germany and Japan are the largest producers."]],
sp:["Maudslay’s micrometer","Henry Maudslay built a bench micrometer he called the ‘Lord Chancellor’ because its judgments were final; it could measure to about a ten-thousandth of an inch."],
g:[["Lathe","A machine that spins a workpiece against a tool"],["Milling machine","A machine with a rotating cutter"],["CNC","Computer numerical control"],["Interchangeable parts","Identical parts that fit any product"]],
f:["Machine tools are called the machines that make machines.","Maudslay’s lathe made standard screws possible.","CNC machines follow computer code."],
fun:"Henry Maudslay’s workshop trained many of the great engineers of the 19th century, including Joseph Whitworth and James Nasmyth, inventor of the steam hammer."});

ADDART({id:"aluminum",t:"Aluminum",c:"Technology",i:"🥫",w:"Aluminium",r:["metals","periodictable","recycling","flight","hydropower"],
p:[["Once more precious than gold","Aluminum was once rare and expensive."],["Light and strong","It is used in planes, cans, cars and buildings."],["Endlessly recyclable","Recycling aluminum saves about 95 percent of the energy."]],
l:"Aluminum is a lightweight, silvery metal and the most abundant metal in Earth’s crust, about 8 percent by weight. It is never found pure in nature but in minerals, mainly bauxite ore. Because extracting it was extremely difficult, aluminum was once more valuable than gold: Napoleon III reportedly served his most honored guests with aluminum cutlery, and a small aluminum pyramid was placed on top of the Washington Monument in 1884 as a precious metal. In 1886, Charles Martin Hall in the United States and Paul Héroult in France independently invented the electrolytic process for producing aluminum cheaply, and the price collapsed. Aluminum is light, about a third of the weight of steel, resists corrosion because it forms a protective oxide layer, conducts electricity and heat well and is easily shaped. It is used in aircraft, cars, drink cans, foil, window frames, power lines, smartphones and cooking pans. Producing new aluminum uses huge amounts of electricity, but recycling it uses about 5 percent of the energy, and it can be recycled endlessly. China produces more than half of the world’s aluminum.",
s:[["The Hall–Héroult process","Alumina, refined from bauxite, is dissolved in molten cryolite at about 950°C, and a powerful electric current separates the aluminum, which collects at the bottom of the cell. Smelters are often built near cheap electricity, such as hydroelectric dams in Iceland, Canada and Norway."],["Aluminum or aluminium?","Chemist Humphry Davy named the metal ‘alumium’ and then ‘aluminum’ around 1808–1812. British scientists later preferred ‘aluminium’ to match elements like sodium and potassium. Americans kept ‘aluminum’, and both spellings are accepted."],["Aluminum in flight","The Wright brothers’ 1903 engine used an aluminum crankcase to save weight. Aluminum alloys made modern aircraft possible; about 80 percent of a typical airliner’s structure has been aluminum, though newer planes use more carbon composites."],["Recycling","Aluminum cans can be recycled and back on store shelves in about 60 days. About three-quarters of all aluminum ever produced is still in use. Recycling saves energy, reduces mining and cuts greenhouse gas emissions."]],
k:[["Share of Earth’s crust","About 8%"],["Main ore","Bauxite"],["Cheap production process","Hall–Héroult, 1886"],["Weight vs steel","About one-third"],["Energy saved by recycling","About 95%"]],
d:[["1825","Hans Christian Ørsted first produces aluminum"],["1855","Aluminum bars shown at the Paris Exhibition"],["1884","Aluminum cap placed on the Washington Monument"],["1886","Hall and Héroult invent cheap electrolysis"],["1959","First aluminum drink cans"]],
s2:[["Napoleon III’s cutlery","Napoleon III is said to have used aluminum plates and cutlery for his most important guests, while lesser guests used gold. The story shows how valuable aluminum once was."],["Charles Martin Hall","Charles Martin Hall made his discovery in a woodshed laboratory at his family home in Ohio, aged 22, with help from his sister Julia. The company he co-founded became Alcoa."]],
sp:["The Washington Monument’s tip","The 2.8-kilogram aluminum pyramid placed atop the Washington Monument in 1884 was one of the largest pieces of aluminum made at the time and was displayed at Tiffany’s jewelry store before installation."],
g:[["Bauxite","The main ore of aluminum"],["Electrolysis","Splitting compounds using electricity"],["Alloy","A mixture of metals"],["Oxide layer","A thin protective surface coating"]],
f:["Aluminum is the most abundant metal in Earth’s crust.","Aluminum was once more valuable than gold.","Recycling aluminum saves about 95% of the energy."],
fun:"About three-quarters of all the aluminum ever produced is still in use today, thanks to recycling."});

ADDART({id:"adhesives",t:"Glues and Adhesives",c:"Technology",i:"🧴",w:"Adhesive",r:["plastics","molecules","geckos","polymers","constructionmachines"],
p:[["Sticking things together","Adhesives bond materials together."],["Ancient glues","Neanderthals made glue from birch tar over 200,000 years ago."],["Superglue and sticky notes","Some famous adhesives were discovered by accident."]],
l:"Adhesives, or glues, are substances that bond surfaces together. They are among humanity’s oldest technologies: Neanderthals in Europe heated birch bark to produce sticky tar to attach stone points to wooden handles more than 200,000 years ago. Ancient Egyptians used animal glues made from boiled hides and bones in furniture, and natural glues were made from plant starches, tree resins and milk proteins. The 20th century brought synthetic adhesives such as epoxies, which harden by chemical reaction into very strong bonds; cyanoacrylate ‘superglue’, which sets almost instantly by reacting with moisture; hot-melt glues; and pressure-sensitive adhesives used in tapes and labels. Adhesives are used in nearly everything: books, shoes, furniture, cars, aircraft, electronics, medical bandages and construction. Modern aircraft and cars use structural adhesives in place of some rivets and welds to save weight. Scientists study natural adhesives, such as the sticky feet of geckos and the glue of mussels that sticks underwater, to design new materials.",
s:[["How glues stick","Adhesives stick by flowing into tiny gaps in surfaces and hardening, by chemical bonds and by attractive forces between molecules. Glues harden by drying as solvents evaporate, by cooling, or by chemical reactions such as those in two-part epoxies."],["Superglue","Cyanoacrylate was discovered in 1942 by Harry Coover, who was trying to make clear plastic gun sights and rejected it because it stuck to everything. He rediscovered its value in 1951. It was used in the Vietnam War to close wounds, and medical versions are still used in place of stitches."],["Sticky notes","In 1968, 3M scientist Spencer Silver created a weak, reusable adhesive. His colleague Art Fry used it to make bookmarks that would not fall out of his hymn book, leading to Post-it Notes, launched in 1980."],["Gecko-inspired adhesives","Geckos climb walls using millions of tiny hairs on their toes that stick through weak molecular forces. Researchers have created gecko-inspired dry adhesives that can grip and release repeatedly, used in robots and grippers."]],
k:[["Oldest known glue","Neanderthal birch tar, over 200,000 years ago"],["Superglue discovered","Harry Coover, 1942"],["Post-it Notes launched","1980"],["Strong structural glue","Epoxy"],["Inspired by nature","Gecko feet, mussel glue"]],
d:[["c. 200,000 BC","Neanderthals make birch tar glue"],["c. 1500 BC","Egyptians use animal glues in furniture"],["1845","First rubber-based surgical tape"],["1925","Masking tape invented by Richard Drew at 3M"],["1942","Superglue discovered"],["1980","Post-it Notes launched"]],
s2:[["Duct tape","Duct tape, a cloth-backed waterproof tape, was developed during World War II to seal ammunition boxes. Its strength and versatility made it famous, and astronauts used it to repair the Apollo 17 lunar rover’s fender on the Moon."],["Glue-free history","Before modern glues, Japanese woodworkers developed intricate joints that fit together without nails or glue, used in temples and furniture."]],
sp:["Mussel glue","Mussels attach to rocks using threads with a protein-based glue that sticks strongly underwater. Scientists are developing similar adhesives for surgery, where wet surfaces make ordinary glues fail."],
g:[["Adhesive","A substance that sticks things together"],["Epoxy","A strong two-part glue"],["Cyanoacrylate","The chemical in superglue"],["Pressure-sensitive adhesive","A sticky material used on tapes and labels"]],
f:["Neanderthals made glue from birch tar.","Superglue was discovered by accident.","Post-it Notes use a weak, reusable adhesive."],
fun:"Astronauts on Apollo 17 repaired a broken fender on their Moon buggy using duct tape and maps."});

ADDART({id:"teflon",t:"Teflon and Nonstick Coatings",c:"Technology",i:"🍳",w:"Polytetrafluoroethylene",r:["plastics","carbonchem","pollution","spacesuits","metals"],
p:[["Slippery plastic","Teflon is a super-slippery plastic discovered by accident in 1938."],["Nonstick pans","It made eggs slide off frying pans."],["Forever chemicals","Chemicals used to make it have raised serious health and pollution concerns."]],
l:"Polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE), best known by the brand name Teflon, is a plastic made of carbon and fluorine atoms that is extremely slippery, heat-resistant and chemically inert, so almost nothing sticks to it and few chemicals attack it. It was discovered by accident on 6 April 1938 by Roy Plunkett, a chemist at DuPont, when a cylinder of refrigerant gas he expected to use seemed empty but turned out to contain a waxy white powder. PTFE was used during World War II in the Manhattan Project to resist corrosive uranium compounds. In 1954, French engineer Marc Grégoire, encouraged by his wife Colette, found a way to bond PTFE to aluminum, creating the first nonstick frying pans, sold under the Tefal brand. PTFE is also used in electrical insulation, seals, medical implants, waterproof fabrics such as Gore-Tex and spacecraft. However, chemicals called PFAS, including PFOA, once used to manufacture PTFE, persist in the environment and the human body for decades, earning the nickname ‘forever chemicals’; they have been linked to health problems, leading to lawsuits, bans and phase-outs.",
s:[["Why nothing sticks","The fluorine atoms in PTFE form a tight shell around the carbon chain, and the strong carbon–fluorine bonds make the surface unreactive and very low in friction. PTFE has one of the lowest friction coefficients of any solid."],["Nonstick cooking","Nonstick pans are safe at normal cooking temperatures, but overheating an empty pan above about 260°C can break down the coating and release fumes that can cause flu-like symptoms in people and can kill pet birds. Manufacturers advise cooking on low to medium heat and avoiding metal utensils."],["Forever chemicals","PFOA, used to make PTFE for decades, contaminated water supplies near factories. Lawsuits in West Virginia, dramatized in the 2019 film Dark Waters, led to large settlements. PFOA was phased out of US production by 2015, and many countries are restricting other PFAS chemicals."],["The space myth","Teflon is often said to have been invented for the space program, but it was discovered in 1938, long before. It has, however, been used in spacesuits, spacecraft and the Statue of Liberty’s restoration."]],
k:[["Chemical name","Polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE)"],["Discovered","Roy Plunkett, 6 April 1938"],["Nonstick pan","Marc Grégoire, 1954"],["Key property","Very low friction"],["Concern","PFAS ‘forever chemicals’"]],
d:[["1938","Roy Plunkett discovers PTFE"],["1945","DuPont registers the Teflon trademark"],["1954","First nonstick pan made"],["1969","Gore-Tex invented using stretched PTFE"],["2015","PFOA phased out of US production"]],
s2:[["The Teflon president","US Representative Patricia Schroeder called President Ronald Reagan the ‘Teflon president’ in 1983 because scandals seemed not to stick to him. The term has since been applied to other politicians."],["Gore-Tex","In 1969, Bob Gore discovered that rapidly stretching PTFE created a material full of tiny pores that let water vapor escape but kept rain out. Gore-Tex became widely used in outdoor clothing."]],
sp:["How it sticks to the pan","Since nothing sticks to Teflon, attaching it to pans was a challenge. Grégoire roughened aluminum surfaces with acid so that PTFE could grip them mechanically."],
g:[["PTFE","Polytetrafluoroethylene, the chemical in Teflon"],["Inert","Not chemically reactive"],["PFAS","A group of long-lasting fluorinated chemicals"],["Friction","Resistance when surfaces rub"]],
f:["Teflon was discovered by accident in 1938.","The first nonstick pan was made in 1954.","PFAS are called forever chemicals."],
fun:"Teflon is so slippery that geckos, which can climb glass, cannot cling to it."});

ADDART({id:"youtube",t:"YouTube",c:"Technology",i:"▶️",w:"YouTube",r:["streaming","socialmedia","shortvideo","worldwideweb","recommender","recalgorithms","copyrightonline","creatoreconomy"],
p:[["Video for everyone","YouTube lets anyone upload and watch videos."],["Founded in 2005","The first video, ‘Me at the zoo’, was uploaded in April 2005."],["Billions of viewers","YouTube is one of the most visited websites in the world."]],
l:"YouTube is a video-sharing platform where users upload, watch, comment on and share videos. It was founded in February 2005 by three former PayPal employees, Chad Hurley, Steve Chen and Jawed Karim; the first video, ‘Me at the zoo’, an 18-second clip of Karim at the San Diego Zoo, was uploaded on 23 April 2005. YouTube grew explosively, and Google bought it in 2006 for 1.65 billion dollars. Today it has more than 2.5 billion monthly users, and hundreds of hours of video are uploaded every minute, in more than 100 countries and 80 languages. YouTube changed entertainment, music, education and news, allowing ordinary people to become creators with millions of subscribers and earn money from advertising. It hosts music videos, tutorials, lectures, gaming streams, vlogs and ‘Shorts’, short vertical videos introduced in 2020. YouTube’s recommendation algorithm, which suggests videos to watch next, drives most viewing; critics say it can spread misinformation and extreme content, and YouTube has faced criticism over content moderation, copyright and children’s safety.",
s:[["Early growth","YouTube’s easy uploading and embedding of videos on other websites helped it spread. A 2005 Nike advertisement featuring footballer Ronaldinho became one of the first videos to reach a million views. In 2012, ‘Gangnam Style’ by PSY became the first video to pass one billion views."],["Creators","YouTube creators earn money from advertising, memberships, sponsorships and merchandise. Some, such as MrBeast, have hundreds of millions of subscribers. Being a YouTuber has become a popular career aspiration among children."],["Learning","YouTube is widely used for learning, from cooking and repairs to mathematics and languages. Channels such as Khan Academy and Crash Course have reached millions of students."],["Problems","YouTube removes videos that break its rules on violence, hate speech and misinformation, using AI and human reviewers, but critics say harmful content still spreads. In 2019, YouTube paid a 170 million dollar settlement over collecting data from children without parental consent."]],
k:[["Founded","February 2005"],["First video","‘Me at the zoo’, 23 April 2005"],["Bought by Google","2006, for 1.65 billion dollars"],["Monthly users","More than 2.5 billion"],["Short videos","YouTube Shorts (2020)"]],
d:[["2005","YouTube founded; first video uploaded"],["2006","Google buys YouTube"],["2007","YouTube Partner Program shares ad revenue"],["2012","Gangnam Style passes 1 billion views"],["2020","YouTube Shorts launched"]],
s2:[["Baby Shark","‘Baby Shark Dance’, a children’s song video from South Korea, became the most-viewed video on YouTube in 2020, eventually passing 15 billion views."],["YouTube and music","YouTube became the world’s most popular music streaming service by number of users. Many artists, such as Justin Bieber, were discovered through YouTube videos."]],
sp:["‘Me at the zoo’","The first YouTube video shows Jawed Karim in front of elephants, commenting that they have ‘really, really, really long trunks’. It has been viewed more than 300 million times."],
g:[["Upload","To send a file to a website"],["Creator","A person who makes online content"],["Algorithm","Rules a computer uses to make decisions"],["Vlog","A video blog"]],
f:["YouTube’s first video was uploaded in 2005.","Google bought YouTube in 2006.","Baby Shark is YouTube’s most-viewed video."],
fun:"YouTube was originally planned as a video dating site called ‘Tune In Hook Up’ before its founders changed the idea."});

ADDART({id:"wikipedia",t:"Wikipedia",c:"Technology",i:"📚",w:"Wikipedia",r:["encyclopedias","worldwideweb","opensource","searchengines","misinformation","onlinecommunities"],
p:[["The free encyclopedia","Wikipedia is a free online encyclopedia written by volunteers."],["Launched in 2001","It began on 15 January 2001."],["Hundreds of languages","It has millions of articles in more than 300 languages."]],
l:"Wikipedia is a free online encyclopedia written and edited collaboratively by volunteers around the world. It was launched on 15 January 2001 by Jimmy Wales and Larry Sanger, growing out of Nupedia, an earlier expert-written encyclopedia that had produced few articles. Wikipedia uses wiki software, which lets anyone edit pages directly in a web browser, and its content is freely licensed so that anyone can reuse it. It grew rapidly and became one of the most visited websites in the world. By late 2025, the English Wikipedia had about 7 million articles, and Wikipedia editions in more than 340 languages together held more than 66 million articles. Wikipedia is run by the Wikimedia Foundation, a nonprofit organization funded mainly by donations, and carries no advertising. Its articles are expected to follow a neutral point of view and cite reliable published sources. Early critics doubted that an encyclopedia anyone could edit could be accurate, but studies found its quality comparable to traditional encyclopedias in many areas, though vandalism, bias and uneven coverage remain challenges. Wikipedia’s content is widely used to train artificial intelligence and power search results.",
s:[["How it works","Every article has a history page showing all changes and a talk page for discussion. Volunteer editors watch for vandalism, and automated programs called bots revert many obvious problems within seconds. Disputes are resolved through discussion and policies, with experienced volunteers acting as administrators."],["Accuracy","A 2005 study in the journal Nature compared science articles in Wikipedia and Encyclopaedia Britannica and found a similar number of serious errors, though Britannica disputed the study. Wikipedia advises readers to check sources, especially for controversial topics."],["Gaps and bias","Most Wikipedia editors are men from wealthier countries, which affects coverage. Projects such as Women in Red have created hundreds of thousands of biographies of women to address this imbalance, and editors work to improve coverage of Africa, Asia and other regions."],["Wikipedia and AI","Wikipedia is one of the most important sources of text used to train large language models, and its information appears in search engine panels and voice assistants. The Wikimedia Foundation has asked AI companies to credit and support Wikipedia."]],
k:[["Launched","15 January 2001"],["Founders","Jimmy Wales and Larry Sanger"],["English articles","About 7 million (late 2025)"],["Languages","More than 340"],["Run by","Wikimedia Foundation (nonprofit)"]],
d:[["2000","Nupedia launched"],["2001","Wikipedia launched"],["2003","Wikimedia Foundation founded"],["2005","Nature study compares Wikipedia and Britannica"],["2012","English Wikipedia blacks out in protest against proposed US laws"],["2026","Wikipedia celebrates its 25th anniversary"]],
s2:[["The blackout","On 18 January 2012, the English Wikipedia blacked out for 24 hours, along with other websites, to protest proposed US laws, SOPA and PIPA, that critics said threatened free expression online. The bills were shelved soon afterward."],["Britannica’s print end","In 2012, Encyclopaedia Britannica announced it would stop printing its encyclopedia after 244 years, a change often linked to the rise of Wikipedia and online information."]],
sp:["Wiki","The word ‘wiki’ comes from the Hawaiian word for ‘quick’. Ward Cunningham created the first wiki software, WikiWikiWeb, in 1995, naming it after the Wiki Wiki shuttle buses at Honolulu airport."],
g:[["Wiki","A website anyone can edit"],["Vandalism","Deliberately damaging content"],["Neutral point of view","Presenting all significant views fairly"],["Free license","Permission for anyone to reuse content"]],
f:["Wikipedia was launched on 15 January 2001.","English Wikipedia has about 7 million articles.","Wiki means ‘quick’ in Hawaiian."],
fun:"The first edit on Wikipedia, made by Jimmy Wales on its launch day, was simply the text ‘Hello, World!’."});

ADDART({id:"textmessaging",t:"Text Messaging",c:"Technology",i:"💬",w:"SMS",r:["phone","smartphone","emoji","socialmedia","email"],
p:[["Short messages","Text messages are short written messages sent between phones."],["The first text","The first SMS, ‘Merry Christmas’, was sent in 1992."],["Messaging apps","Apps such as WhatsApp now carry most messages."]],
l:"Text messaging is the sending of short written messages between mobile phones and other devices. The Short Message Service (SMS), developed as part of the GSM mobile phone standard in the 1980s, limited messages to 160 characters, a length chosen by engineer Friedhelm Hillebrand after counting the characters in typical postcards and telex messages. The first SMS, ‘Merry Christmas’, was sent from a computer to a phone by British engineer Neil Papworth on 3 December 1992. Texting grew slowly at first but became hugely popular in the late 1990s and 2000s, especially among young people, despite typing letters on number keypads, aided by predictive text such as T9. The Philippines was once called the ‘texting capital of the world’ because of how much its people used SMS. Texting created its own language of abbreviations, such as ‘LOL’ and ‘BRB’, and later emoji. In the 2010s, internet-based messaging apps such as WhatsApp, WeChat, iMessage, Messenger, Telegram and Signal overtook SMS, adding photos, voice messages, video calls and group chats. RCS, a modern successor to SMS, is now supported by Android and Apple phones.",
s:[["160 characters","SMS messages fit into the spare capacity of signaling channels used by mobile networks, which limited their length. Hillebrand typed sentences on a typewriter and found that 160 characters were enough for most short messages. Twitter originally limited posts to 140 characters to fit into an SMS with room for a username."],["Texting language","Texters abbreviated words to save effort and characters: ‘C U L8R’ for ‘see you later’ and ‘GR8’ for ‘great’. Linguists found that such texting did not harm young people’s literacy and often showed creative play with language."],["Messaging apps","WhatsApp, founded in 2009 and bought by Facebook in 2014, offers free encrypted messaging over the internet and has more than 2 billion users. WeChat in China combines messaging with payments and other services. These apps reduced SMS use and costs."],["Texting and safety","Texting while driving greatly increases crash risk, and many countries ban using handheld phones while driving. SMS is also used for emergency alerts, bank verification codes and public health messages."]],
k:[["SMS limit","160 characters"],["First SMS","‘Merry Christmas’, 3 December 1992"],["Sender","Neil Papworth"],["Predictive text","T9 (1990s)"],["Major messaging apps","WhatsApp, WeChat, iMessage, Messenger"]],
d:[["1984","Friedhelm Hillebrand sets the 160-character limit"],["1992","First SMS sent"],["1995","T9 predictive text invented"],["2009","WhatsApp founded"],["2011","Apple introduces iMessage"],["2024","Apple adopts RCS messaging"]],
s2:[["The texting capital","In the early 2000s, Filipinos sent hundreds of millions of texts a day thanks to cheap SMS and prepaid phones. Text messages helped organize the protests that led to the fall of President Joseph Estrada in 2001."],["Thumb generation","Researchers noticed young texters using their thumbs more skillfully than older people, sometimes pressing doorbells with their thumbs, and called them the ‘thumb generation’."]],
sp:["The first text","Neil Papworth sent the first text message from a computer because phones could not yet type messages. The recipient, Vodafone director Richard Jarvis, was at a Christmas party and could not reply. The text was sold as an NFT in 2021."],
g:[["SMS","Short Message Service"],["Predictive text","Software that guesses the word being typed"],["Encryption","Scrambling messages so only recipients can read them"],["RCS","Rich Communication Services, a successor to SMS"]],
f:["The first text message was ‘Merry Christmas’.","SMS messages are limited to 160 characters.","WhatsApp has more than 2 billion users."],
fun:"The first-ever text message was sold as a digital collectible in 2021 for about 107,000 euros."});

ADDART({id:"lithium",t:"Lithium",c:"Technology",i:"🔋",w:"Lithium",r:["battery","ev","periodictable","chilecountry","australiacountry","saltflats"],
p:[["The lightest metal","Lithium is the lightest metal and the third element in the periodic table."],["Battery power","Lithium-ion batteries power phones, laptops and electric cars."],["White gold","Demand for lithium has soared with electric vehicles."]],
l:"Lithium is a soft, silvery-white metal and the lightest of all metals, with atomic number 3. It is so reactive that it is never found pure in nature and must be stored in oil. Swedish chemist Johan August Arfwedson discovered it in 1817 in a mineral called petalite. Lithium is used in lubricating greases, glass and ceramics, medicines and, most importantly today, rechargeable lithium-ion batteries, which power smartphones, laptops, electric vehicles and grid energy storage. Lithium-ion batteries were developed by John Goodenough, Stanley Whittingham and Akira Yoshino, who shared the 2019 Nobel Prize in Chemistry, and Sony sold the first commercial ones in 1991. Lithium is mined from hard rock, mainly in Australia, and extracted from salty brines in the high deserts of Chile, Argentina and Bolivia, the ‘Lithium Triangle’; China refines most of the world’s lithium. Growing demand for electric vehicles has made lithium a strategically important resource, sometimes called ‘white gold’, and prices have swung wildly. Mining raises concerns about water use, pollution and the rights of local and Indigenous communities.",
s:[["Lithium-ion batteries","In a lithium-ion battery, lithium ions move from the negative electrode to the positive electrode through an electrolyte as the battery discharges, and back again when charging. Lithium’s lightness and high energy make these batteries far lighter than earlier types. Damaged batteries can overheat and catch fire, so they need careful design."],["Brines and mines","In Chile’s Atacama Desert, brine is pumped into huge evaporation ponds, where the sun concentrates the lithium over many months. Hard-rock mines in Australia dig a mineral called spodumene. New methods aim to extract lithium directly from brines with less water."],["Lithium in medicine","Lithium salts have been used since 1949 to treat bipolar disorder, after Australian psychiatrist John Cade discovered their calming effect. Lithium remains one of the most effective mood-stabilizing medicines, though doses must be carefully monitored."],["Recycling","Recycling lithium from used batteries is becoming important to reduce mining. Companies recover lithium, cobalt, nickel and other metals from old batteries, and some countries now require battery recycling."]],
k:[["Atomic number","3"],["Discovered","Johan August Arfwedson, 1817"],["Main use","Lithium-ion batteries"],["Major producers","Australia, Chile, China, Argentina"],["Nobel Prize","2019 (lithium-ion batteries)"]],
d:[["1817","Lithium discovered"],["1949","John Cade discovers lithium’s effect on bipolar disorder"],["1970s","Whittingham develops the first lithium battery concepts"],["1991","Sony sells the first lithium-ion batteries"],["2019","Nobel Prize for lithium-ion batteries"]],
s2:[["The Lithium Triangle","Chile, Argentina and Bolivia hold more than half of the world’s known lithium resources in their salt flats. Bolivia’s Salar de Uyuni, the world’s largest salt flat, contains huge reserves that have been little exploited."],["Lithium from the Big Bang","Lithium is one of only three elements, with hydrogen and helium, made in significant amounts in the Big Bang. Astronomers measure lithium in old stars to test theories of the early universe."]],
sp:["Lighter than water","Lithium is so light that it floats on water, but it reacts with it, fizzing and producing hydrogen gas. It is the least dense metal."],
g:[["Lithium-ion battery","A rechargeable battery using lithium ions"],["Brine","Very salty water"],["Electrolyte","A substance that lets ions move"],["Spodumene","A lithium-bearing mineral"]],
f:["Lithium is the lightest metal.","Lithium-ion batteries won the 2019 Nobel Prize.","Lithium floats on water."],
fun:"Lithium is soft enough to cut with a knife and light enough to float on oil."});

ADDART({id:"faxmachines",t:"Fax Machines",c:"Technology",i:"📠",w:"Fax",r:["telegraph","phone","printers","email","japancountry"],
p:[["Sending pages by phone","Fax machines send copies of documents over telephone lines."],["Older than the telephone","The first fax patent dates from 1843."],["Still in use","Faxes remain common in some offices and hospitals."]],
l:"A fax machine, short for facsimile, scans a paper document, converts the image into signals and sends it over a telephone line to another fax machine, which prints a copy. Surprisingly, the idea is older than the telephone: Scottish inventor Alexander Bain patented a method of transmitting images over telegraph wires in 1843, and Italian priest Giovanni Caselli’s pantelegraph provided a commercial fax service between Paris and Lyon from 1865. In the 20th century, newspapers and police used wirephoto and fax systems to send photographs. Fax machines became common in offices in the 1970s and 1980s after international standards made machines compatible and prices fell, especially in Japan, where faxing handwritten Japanese characters was easier than typing them. By the 1990s, fax machines were everywhere, but email, scanning and electronic signatures largely replaced them. Faxes are still used in some places, including Japanese offices, legal firms and hospitals, where they are trusted for security and legal records, although governments have tried to phase them out.",
s:[["How a fax works","The sending machine scans the page line by line, dividing it into tiny dots that are black or white. These are encoded as sounds and sent through the phone line; the receiving machine decodes them and prints the dots. The screeching sounds of fax machines connecting are the machines negotiating speed and settings."],["Early faxes","Bain’s device used pendulums synchronized at each end. Caselli’s pantelegraph sent handwriting and drawings, mainly for checking signatures on bank documents. In the 1920s, newspapers sent photos across oceans by radio fax."],["The fax boom","International standards from the 1970s and 1980s let machines from different makers communicate. Thermal paper faxes, which faded over time, were common. By the late 1980s, millions of businesses relied on faxes for orders, contracts and memos."],["Japan and faxes","Japan was a center of fax manufacturing, and faxes remained in wide use there long after other countries switched to email. During the COVID-19 pandemic, reports of health data being faxed prompted the government to push digital reforms."]],
k:[["First patent","Alexander Bain, 1843"],["First commercial service","Pantelegraph, Paris–Lyon, 1865"],["Office boom","1970s–1990s"],["Transmission","Over telephone lines"],["Still used in","Some offices, hospitals and law firms"]],
d:[["1843","Alexander Bain patents a fax device"],["1865","Pantelegraph service between Paris and Lyon"],["1924","Wirephotos sent by newspapers"],["1964","Xerox introduces a fax machine for telephone lines"],["1980","International fax standard adopted"],["1990s","Fax use peaks"]],
s2:[["Faxes and the fall of communism","During the 1989 Tiananmen Square protests and the collapse of communist regimes in Eastern Europe, fax machines helped activists send news and information past censors."],["Fax in medicine","In the United Kingdom, the National Health Service was told in 2018 to stop using fax machines by 2020, as it was one of the world’s largest buyers of them."]],
sp:["A sound of the past","The modem sounds of fax machines and dial-up internet, a series of beeps, hisses and screeches, are remembered fondly by many people as the soundtrack of early digital communication."],
g:[["Facsimile","An exact copy"],["Scanner","A device that converts paper images into digital data"],["Thermal paper","Paper that darkens when heated"],["Modem","A device that converts data into sound signals"]],
f:["The first fax patent came in 1843.","Fax machines predate the telephone.","Faxes are still used in some hospitals and offices."],
fun:"The fax machine was invented 33 years before the telephone."});
